--TEST--
`Core\Arr::from` and a `foreach` drive one sequence the same way, agreeing on the entries seen and on the advances made at every prefix of it, over all three shapes — spec § 2, ADR 0053 §§ 1 and 3
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/arr.rs:2527`'s doc says `{limit: n}` stops the *drive*
// rather than truncating the answer, and that the cursor behind all three
// shapes is driven by name in `nvs_runtime::sequence` — by the same two calls
// a `foreach` makes. The two existing cases pin one side each:
// `arr-from-drains-a-sequence` compares finished arrays, and
// `arr-from-limit-bounds-the-drive-on-both-sides` counts `from`'s own advances
// against numbers written into the case. What is left is the *agreement*: one
// source walked by `foreach` and drained by `from`, asserting the two see the
// same entries after the same number of advances at every prefix — so a `from`
// that grew a drive of its own fails here while each of its own lines still
// reads correctly on its own.

// ADR 0053 § 1 gives an `Iterator<T>` exactly `advance()` and `current()`, so
// the count of the first is the whole of what a program can observe about a
// drive. The values carry a duplicate pair and a zero: a drive that collapsed
// repeats, or that read a `0` as the end of the sequence, agrees with nothing
// below.
class Counted implements Iterator<int> {
    private array<int> $values;
    private int $at;
    public uint $advances;

    public function constructor() {
        $this->values = [7, 7, 9, 4, 0];
        $this->at = -1;
        $this->advances = 0;
    }

    public function advance(): bool {
        $this->advances = $this->advances + 1;
        $this->at = $this->at + 1;
        return $this->at < 5;
    }

    public function current(): int {
        return $this->values[$this->at];
    }
}

// ADR 0053 § 1's second shape: a thing that produces a fresh cursor, which
// `foreach` and `from` each ask for exactly once.
class Numbers implements Iterable<int> {
    public function iterate(): Iterator<int> {
        return new Counted();
    }
}

// One `foreach`, observed at every prefix rather than only at its end. After
// the k-th element is bound, `advances` is exactly k; the entry appended after
// the loop is the state past the last element, where the advance that *reported
// exhaustion* has happened and no element came back from it. Nothing about
// `Core\Arr::from` is consulted to build this table — that is what makes it a
// second opinion rather than a restatement.
var $walked = new Counted();
array<int> $sofar = [];
array<string> $seenAt = ["[]"];
array<uint> $advancesAt = [0];
foreach ($walked as int $v) {
    $sofar[] = $v;
    $seenAt[] = Core\Json::encode($sofar);
    $advancesAt[] = $walked->advances;
}
$seenAt[] = Core\Json::encode($sofar);
$advancesAt[] = $walked->advances;

// The agreement itself, at all seven prefixes of a five-element sequence: the
// two below the count, the exact count, and the one past it where the source
// stops the drive instead of the limit. A fresh cursor per question, since a
// cursor is spent once drained.
//
// The third counter is the control. Asked against the *next* prefix, the same
// comparison must agree nowhere at all — otherwise `7 7 7` would be what a
// table of seven identical cells prints too, and the case would pass over a
// `from` that answered the same array for every limit.
int $asked = 0;
int $entryAgrees = 0;
int $driveAgrees = 0;
int $offByOneAgrees = 0;
uint $n = 0;
while ($n < 7) {
    $asked = $asked + 1;
    var $c = new Counted();
    string $got = Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::from($c, {limit: $n}));
    if ($got == $seenAt[$n]) {
        $entryAgrees = $entryAgrees + 1;
    }
    if ($c->advances == $advancesAt[$n]) {
        $driveAgrees = $driveAgrees + 1;
    }
    if ($n < 5 && $got == $seenAt[$n + 1]) {
        $offByOneAgrees = $offByOneAgrees + 1;
    }
    $n = $n + 1;
}
echo $asked as string, " ", $entryAgrees as string, " ", $driveAgrees as string,
     " ", $offByOneAgrees as string, "\n";

// The table both counters were read against, said out loud so a later change to
// the drive shows up as this line moving rather than as a count staying at 7.
echo Core\Json::encode($seenAt), "\n";
echo Core\Json::encode($advancesAt), "\n";

// The same agreement with no limit written at all: the drive that only the
// source stops makes the same six advances the `foreach` made, and answers what
// the `foreach` collected.
var $free = new Counted();
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::from($free)), " ", $free->advances as string, " ",
     Core\Json::encode($sofar), " ", $walked->advances as string, "\n";

// Shape two. Each of the two asks `iterate()` once and drives what it gets, so
// the agreement is over the entries; the advance counts live inside cursors
// neither caller keeps.
var $nums = new Numbers();
array<int> $iterableWalk = [];
foreach ($nums as int $v2) {
    $iterableWalk[] = $v2;
}
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::from($nums)), " ", Core\Json::encode($iterableWalk), "\n";

// Shape three, where the two callers differ on purpose and the agreement says
// exactly where: § 2 makes `from`'s answer a list whichever shape went in, and
// a `foreach` binds each key beside its value. So the two agree on the
// *values*, in order, and the keys are the part `from` drops.
array<int> $keyed = ["w" => 7, "x" => 7, "y" => 9, "z" => 4, "" => 0];
array<int> $arrayWalk = [];
array<string> $arrayKeys = [];
foreach ($keyed as string $k => int $v3) {
    $arrayWalk[] = $v3;
    $arrayKeys[] = $k;
}
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::from($keyed)), " ", Core\Json::encode($arrayWalk), " ",
     Core\Json::encode(Core\Arr::keys($keyed)), " ", Core\Json::encode($arrayKeys), "\n";
--EXPECT--
7 7 7 0
["[]","[7]","[7,7]","[7,7,9]","[7,7,9,4]","[7,7,9,4,0]","[7,7,9,4,0]"]
[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]
[7,7,9,4,0] 6 [7,7,9,4,0] 6
[7,7,9,4,0] [7,7,9,4,0]
[7,7,9,4,0] [7,7,9,4,0] ["w","x","y","z",""] ["w","x","y","z",""]
